{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And he said, etc. — this is the thing, etc.</b> He said this to inform them, so that they would not rebel against the matter like Korah and his assembly, thinking that he was acting of his own heart and will. From now on Korah has no claim that he acted unwittingly, for he informed him at the time of the act that this thing is a commandment of HaShem and not from the heart and will of Moses." "Further, it intends according to the statement of the Sages, whose memory is for a blessing (Shemot Rabbah 2:6; Zevachim 102a), that Moses was a king, as it is written (Deuteronomy 33:5) ״וַיְהִי בִישֻׁרוּן מֶלֶךְ״, and we learned (Ketubot 17a): a king who waives his honor — his honor is not waived. Now Moses was performing actions that are forbidden to do even to a Hebrew slave for his master — to wash them in water, etc., over their entire body and the entire order of the act — and there is no greater indignity than this. Therefore he prefaced by saying <b>this is the thing that HaShem commanded</b> — from the mouth of HaShem it was said to me to perform it, and specifically this and not another, for the king is forbidden to do thus even if he wishes to waive, for specifically this thing — since the very mouth that decreed that a king may not waive his honor is what permitted and commanded me to perform." "Further, it intends according to what the Sages, whose memory is for a blessing, said in Torat Kohanim on the verse ״וַיִּרְחַץ אוֹתָם בַּמָּיִם״: at that moment they merited sanctification of hands and feet and the immersion of Yom Kippur — end of quote. And this is the verse's saying ״this is the thing <b>that HaShem commanded to do</b>״ for the generations, for through this they will merit sanctification of hands and feet and immersion on Yom Kippur for the generations:"], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}],